<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Manage a Club – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage a Club</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Distinguish between facts and opinions in texts on everyday subjects. Reading: Derive the meaning of simple unknown words from short texts.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Can distinguish between facts and opinions in announcements, posters, or newsletters about school events (e.g., Fact: "The music club practices every Thursday." Opinion: "The music club is the most fun because it's energetic."). Can find the meaning of unknown words in a text about club activities (e.g., "I think in this sentence, 'engage' means to participate actively."). Can separate factual and opinion-based statements in a list of club benefits (e.g., Fact: "It is beneficial to join social clubs." Opinion: "I believe joining a club improves your social skills.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various short announcements or social media posts from different school clubs (e.g., "Drama Club auditions on Tuesday!", "Photography Club exhibition next week – see our amazing project!", "Join the Debate Team and discuss current issues!"). Students, in pairs, quickly scan the texts and identify the name of the club and one activity. The teacher asks, "What type of club would you like to join based on these announcements?" to activate prior knowledge of club preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for keywords and main ideas. Using one of the warm-up texts, the teacher demonstrates distinguishing a fact from an opinion (e.g., "The club meets in room 305" vs. "This is the most exciting club on campus!"). The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like /pr/ in "project" and /tr/ in "treasure," emphasizing the stress on the third syllable from the end in verbs ending in -ate (e.g., "delegate," "coordinate").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'fact' and 'opinion'.</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'The art club meets on Mondays' a fact or an opinion? How do you know?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The coding club is the best club in school,' is that a fact or an opinion? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short article or online announcement titled "Effective Strategies for Managing a School Club." This text will include descriptions of club `structure`, `responsibilities`, `goals`, `meetings`, and `activities`, using simple present, simple past (e.g., "Last year, the drama club created an amazing project"), and imperatives (e.g., "Set goals," "Let's plan activities"). It will also include words like `members`, `team`, `event`, `community`, `feedback`, `suggestions`, `participation`, `leadership`, `manage`, `organize`, `create`, `engage`, `discuss`, `coordinate`, `assign`, `delegate`, `evaluate`, `contribute`, `participate`, `schedule`, `arrange`, `support`, `facilitate`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding the main ideas and specific details in short texts related to school clubs and management. Identifying factual information versus subjective opinions within the text. Deriving meaning of simple unknown words from context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly (1-2 minutes) and answer: "What is the main purpose of this article? (a) To advertise a new club, (b) To explain how to run a club well, (c) To describe different types of clubs."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading (Fact vs. Opinion):</b> Students reread the article more carefully. The teacher provides a list of sentences related to the text, and students must label each sentence as "Fact" or "Opinion." Example: "The article states that clear goals are important for a club's success." (Fact) vs. "I believe that joining a club improves your social skills." (Opinion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary in Context:</b> Students identify three words in the text they don't know well. They then use context clues within the sentences to guess the meaning of these words (e.g., for 'engage': "Club members must engage actively in all tasks to contribute to the project's success. This means they need to participate fully.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice distinguishing facts and opinions, and inferring meaning from context in texts about club management. They will use `simple present` for descriptions, `imperatives` for club instructions, and `simple past` for past club experiences, integrating vocabulary such as `goals`, `members`, `responsibilities`, `manage`, `organize`, `participate`, `feedback`, `suggestions`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Fact/Opinion Sort):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with 8-10 sentences taken directly from the "Effective Strategies for Managing a School Club" article or similar texts. Students individually read each sentence and categorize it as either a "Fact" or an "Opinion" in a T-chart. For example: "The book club meets every Tuesday at 3 PM." (Fact) vs. "Reading books is the most relaxing activity a club can offer." (Opinion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Contextual Inference):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives them 3-4 sentences from the reading text, each containing a target vocabulary word (e.g., `delegate`, `coordinate`, `engage`). For each word, students must: a) explain its meaning in their own words based on the sentence, and b) identify which words or phrases in the sentence helped them infer the meaning. For example, for "We need to `delegate` tasks among our `team` members to ensure everyone `contributes`," students infer `delegate` means to assign tasks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Justify Your Choice):</b> In their pairs, students discuss 3-4 sentences from the initial "Fact/Opinion Sort" activity. One student states their classification (fact or opinion), and the other student asks "Why?" or "How do you know?". They must `respond` clearly, using phrases like "It's a fact because it can be proven" or "It's an opinion because it expresses a personal belief." They can also discuss how to `negotiate` roles if they disagree.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Club Profile Analysis.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives two different short "Club Profile" texts (e.g., an announcement for a "Creative Writing Club" and a "Community Service Club"). These profiles contain both factual information (meeting times, requirements, types of `activities`) and opinions (e.g., "The most rewarding `project` is helping the `community`," "Our `members` find great joy in `participating`").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads both club profiles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For each club, they must collectively create a "Club Snapshot" in a T-chart format, identifying at least 3 facts and 3 opinions about the club. They should `discuss` and `coordinate` their findings, `assign` roles if necessary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must also identify 2-3 new or challenging vocabulary words from the texts and, as a group, `derive` their meaning from context. They will `negotiate` the best explanation for each word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group chooses one club profile and briefly presents their "Club Snapshot" to another group, explaining their classification of facts and opinions, and their interpretation of new vocabulary. They `respond` to `feedback` or questions from the other group.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a review of the "Club Snapshot" T-charts and vocabulary explanations. Teacher pays attention to student use of `simple present`, `simple past` (e.g., narrating past experiences), and `imperatives` during group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their completed "Club Snapshot" T-chart (listing facts and opinions for both clubs) and their list of 2-3 inferred vocabulary words with contextual definitions. Additionally, one member from each group orally shares one fact, one opinion, and one inferred word during a whole-class wrap-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in distinguishing facts from opinions in the submitted T-chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Plausibility of derived meaning for unknown words from context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., `members`, `activities`, `goals`, `responsibilities`, `manage`, `organize`, `participate`) in oral explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation during the oral share.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The most challenging part of distinguishing facts from opinions today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One strategy I used to figure out the meaning of a new word was..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What important information did I learn about managing or choosing a club by distinguishing facts and opinions today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the ability to derive meaning from context help me in other subjects or in real life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., the fact/opinion sorting activity, student engagement in inferring word meanings, the group discussion about club profiles).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face? (e.g., identifying subtle opinions, unfamiliar vocabulary, difficulty `coordinating` group work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for Lesson 3? (e.g., more explicit examples of opinion markers, providing a list of common opinion adjectives, pairing weaker readers with stronger ones). I need to ensure students are comfortable discussing their `club preferences` and narrating `stories` about past club experiences, which will be the focus for the speaking lesson.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the factual and opinion-based information they've learned about clubs to `discuss` and `negotiate` roles and `responsibilities` for their own imagined club. The `vocabulary` and `grammatical structures` (simple present, simple past, imperatives) practiced today will be essential for their oral `interaction` and `narrating stories` about club experiences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>